.Dd August 17, 2026
.Dt SOWA-SSHD-KEYGEN 8
.Os Sowa
.Sh NAME
.Nm sowa-sshd-keygen
.Nd create the sshd host key on the running system
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Sh DESCRIPTION
A host key identifies a machine, so shipping one in the image would give every
Sowa installation the same identity: anything that trusted one would trust all
of them, and the first machine to be installed would be able to impersonate
every machine installed after it.
The image therefore carries no host key at all, and the build refuses to make
one that does.
.Pp
.Nm
is what creates it instead.
.Pa /etc/rc.d/init.d/sshd
runs it before starting the daemon, so a fresh installation generates its own
key at the first boot that starts SSH.
.Pp
It creates the Ed25519 key that
.Pa /etc/ssh/sshd_config
names, and nothing else.
.Ic ssh-keygen -A
adds the remaining types if a client that cannot speak Ed25519 ever needs one.
The key is commented
.Ar root@ Ns Ar hostname ,
read from
.Pa /etc/hostname .
.Pp
It takes no options and is a no-op once the key exists, so running it by hand is
never wrong and never regenerates anything.
Deleting the key and running it again is how a machine is given a new identity -
after cloning a disk image, for instance, where every copy would otherwise
answer with the same fingerprint.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/etc/ssh/ssh_host_ed25519_key.pub" -compact
.It Pa /etc/ssh/ssh_host_ed25519_key
the private key, mode 0600.
.It Pa /etc/ssh/ssh_host_ed25519_key.pub
the public half, mode 0644.
.It Pa /etc/rc.d/init.d/sshd
what runs this.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh EXAMPLES
Give a cloned machine an identity of its own:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
rm -f /etc/ssh/ssh_host_*
sowa-sshd-keygen
service sshd restart
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ssh-keygen 1 ,
.Xr sshd_config 5 ,
.Xr service 8 ,
.Xr sshd 8
